 "result": "All in `src/tessel/views.py`:\n\n1. **`VerbView.__init_subclass__`** (line 165) runs when you define a subclass. It scans for `get`, `post`, etc. (the names in `http_method_funcs`), including those inherited from bases, and sets `cls.methods = {\"GET\", \"POST\"}`.\n2. **`View.to_view_func(name, ...)`** (line 86) is a classmethod that builds a plain `view(**kwargs)` function. It instantiates the class per request, or once if `init_every_request` is `False`, and calls `self.run_view(**kwargs)` through `active_app.ensure_sync`. Class `decorators` are applied here. It then copies `view_class`, `methods` and `provide_automatic_options` onto the function so route registration can read them. You register it with `app.attach_route_rule(path, view_func=CounterAPI.to_view_func(\"counter\"))`.\n3. **`VerbView.run_view`** (line 182) does the per-method routing. It looks up `getattr(self, request.method.lower())`, falls back to `get` for `HEAD` when there is no `head` method, and calls the handler with the URL kwargs.\n\n`View` is the base class, and its `run_view` leaves the method dispatch to subclasses. `VerbView` is the subclass that implements it for `get()` and `post()`.",
